1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,013
Lego bricks snap together, or break apart.

2
00:00:03,013 --> 00:00:06,594
In a team, a stronger player
can take another player's place.

3
00:00:06,994 --> 00:00:08,820
Redox reactions behave the same way.

4
00:00:08,820 --> 00:00:10,343
They come in a few families.

5
00:00:10,793 --> 00:00:14,102
Aarav waits in the school corridor
before his chemistry test.

6
00:00:14,102 --> 00:00:17,139
His notes have twenty reactions,
all looking different.

7
00:00:17,489 --> 00:00:19,347
He wants a fast way to sort them.

8
00:00:19,847 --> 00:00:24,968
By the end, you will sort any redox
reaction into its family, using oxidation

9
00:00:24,968 --> 00:00:25,868
numbers.

10
00:00:26,354 --> 00:00:29,016
First, is a reaction redox at all?

11
00:00:29,016 --> 00:00:33,220
Write the oxidation number of
every atom, before and after.

12
00:00:33,570 --> 00:00:37,696
If one number goes up and
another goes down, it is redox.

13
00:00:38,096 --> 00:00:42,903
If no number changes, it is not
redox, however dramatic it looks.

14
00:00:43,503 --> 00:00:45,153
Family one, combination.

15
00:00:45,153 --> 00:00:49,780
Two substances join to make one, and
at least one of them is an element.

16
00:00:50,130 --> 00:00:53,427
Carbon burns in oxygen
to give carbon dioxide.

17
00:00:53,427 --> 00:00:56,060
Carbon goes from zero to plus four.

18
00:00:56,060 --> 00:00:58,693
Oxygen goes from zero to minus two.

19
00:00:59,093 --> 00:01:01,115
Family two, decomposition.

20
00:01:01,115 --> 00:01:03,998
One compound breaks into smaller pieces.

21
00:01:03,998 --> 00:01:07,500
Electricity splits water
into hydrogen and oxygen.

22
00:01:07,900 --> 00:01:08,966
But careful.

23
00:01:08,966 --> 00:01:13,756
Heated calcium carbonate gives calcium
oxide and carbon dioxide, and no

24
00:01:13,756 --> 00:01:14,987
number changes.

25
00:01:14,987 --> 00:01:16,800
That one is not redox.

26
00:01:17,450 --> 00:01:19,327
Family three, displacement.

27
00:01:19,327 --> 00:01:22,303
One element pushes
another out of a compound.

28
00:01:22,303 --> 00:01:24,559
Let's watch on our molecule bench.

29
00:01:24,909 --> 00:01:29,458
Chlorine gas bubbles into a colourless
solution of potassium bromide.

30
00:01:29,858 --> 00:01:33,542
Each chlorine atom snatches one
electron from a bromide ion.

31
00:01:33,542 --> 00:01:35,935
Chlorine becomes chloride, minus one.

32
00:01:36,335 --> 00:01:40,111
The bromide ions lose their electrons,
pair up, and become bromine.

33
00:01:40,111 --> 00:01:41,627
The solution turns orange.

34
00:01:42,027 --> 00:01:45,664
Chlorine pushed bromine out, because
chlorine pulls electrons harder.

35
00:01:45,664 --> 00:01:47,558
Fluorine is the strongest of all.

36
00:01:48,158 --> 00:01:50,518
Metals can push each other out too.

37
00:01:50,518 --> 00:01:53,979
Here is the activity series,
most active at the top.

38
00:01:54,329 --> 00:01:59,332
Potassium, sodium, calcium, magnesium,
aluminium, zinc, iron, lead, then

39
00:01:59,332 --> 00:02:02,289
hydrogen, copper, mercury, silver, gold.

40
00:02:03,039 --> 00:02:06,160
A metal higher up pushes
out a metal lower down.

41
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:08,820
Zinc pushes copper out of copper sulphate.

42
00:02:08,820 --> 00:02:10,693
Copper cannot push zinc out.

43
00:02:11,443 --> 00:02:14,630
Metals above hydrogen push
hydrogen out of acids.

44
00:02:14,630 --> 00:02:17,556
Zinc in hydrochloric
acid gives hydrogen gas.

45
00:02:17,556 --> 00:02:20,032
Sodium does it even with cold water.

46
00:02:20,782 --> 00:02:22,863
Family four, disproportionation.

47
00:02:22,863 --> 00:02:24,982
A long word for a simple idea.

48
00:02:25,332 --> 00:02:29,637
One element, in one substance,
goes up and down at the same time.

49
00:02:30,037 --> 00:02:32,196
Chlorine in cold sodium hydroxide.

50
00:02:32,196 --> 00:02:33,818
Chlorine starts at zero.

51
00:02:33,818 --> 00:02:36,024
Some becomes chloride, minus one.

52
00:02:36,024 --> 00:02:38,375
Some becomes hypochlorite, plus one.

53
00:02:38,775 --> 00:02:40,444
The opposite also happens.

54
00:02:40,444 --> 00:02:44,398
Hydrogen sulphide, minus two,
meets sulphur dioxide, plus four.

55
00:02:44,398 --> 00:02:46,161
Both become sulphur, zero.

56
00:02:46,161 --> 00:02:47,793
They meet in the middle.

57
00:02:47,793 --> 00:02:49,835
This is called comproportionation.

58
00:02:50,435 --> 00:02:52,979
Now compare two families side by side.

59
00:02:52,979 --> 00:02:55,860
Decomposition breaks one
compound into pieces.

60
00:02:56,260 --> 00:03:00,736
Disproportionation is about one element
splitting its score, some up and

61
00:03:00,736 --> 00:03:01,636
some down.

62
00:03:02,019 --> 00:03:03,381
Pause and think.

63
00:03:03,381 --> 00:03:06,723
Hydrogen peroxide breaks
into water and oxygen.

64
00:03:06,723 --> 00:03:08,926
Is it also disproportionation?

65
00:03:12,126 --> 00:03:13,026
Yes!

66
00:03:12,628 --> 00:03:14,498
Oxygen starts at minus one.

67
00:03:14,498 --> 00:03:16,939
In water it goes down to minus two.

68
00:03:16,939 --> 00:03:19,282
In oxygen gas it goes up to zero.

69
00:03:19,282 --> 00:03:21,191
One reaction, two families.

70
00:03:21,841 --> 00:03:22,786
Remember this.

71
00:03:22,786 --> 00:03:25,685
Combine, break, push
out, or split the score.

72
00:03:25,685 --> 00:03:28,574
Four families, all checked
with oxidation numbers.

73
00:03:29,274 --> 00:03:30,824
Now, one worked example.

74
00:03:30,824 --> 00:03:32,063
Sort four reactions.

75
00:03:32,363 --> 00:03:33,263
One.

76
00:03:32,963 --> 00:03:36,865
Three magnesium plus nitrogen
give magnesium nitride.

77
00:03:36,865 --> 00:03:41,696
Magnesium goes zero to plus two,
nitrogen zero to minus three.

78
00:03:41,696 --> 00:03:42,766
Combination.

79
00:03:43,516 --> 00:03:44,416
Two.

80
00:03:44,057 --> 00:03:48,067
Potassium chlorate breaks into
potassium chloride and oxygen.

81
00:03:48,067 --> 00:03:52,544
Chlorine goes plus five to minus
one, oxygen minus two to zero.

82
00:03:52,544 --> 00:03:53,615
Decomposition.

83
00:03:54,365 --> 00:03:55,265
Three.

84
00:03:55,054 --> 00:03:59,278
Iron in hydrochloric acid gives
iron chloride and hydrogen.

85
00:03:59,278 --> 00:04:01,130
Iron is above hydrogen.

86
00:04:01,130 --> 00:04:02,215
Displacement.

87
00:04:02,965 --> 00:04:03,865
Four.

88
00:04:03,512 --> 00:04:06,857
Phosphorus in alkali gives
phosphine and hypophosphite.

89
00:04:06,857 --> 00:04:10,681
Phosphorus goes from zero to
minus three, and to plus one.

90
00:04:10,681 --> 00:04:11,913
Disproportionation.

91
00:04:12,563 --> 00:04:14,027
Now, the exam trap.

92
00:04:14,377 --> 00:04:17,679
Students call every
decomposition a redox reaction.

93
00:04:17,679 --> 00:04:21,667
But calcium carbonate breaking
up changes no oxidation number.

94
00:04:22,067 --> 00:04:23,855
Always check the numbers.

95
00:04:23,855 --> 00:04:25,489
No change, no redox.

96
00:04:26,139 --> 00:04:27,459
Where is this asked?

97
00:04:27,459 --> 00:04:30,319
Boards ask you to classify
reactions with reasons.

98
00:04:30,319 --> 00:04:33,392
Entrance tests ask which
reaction is disproportionation.

99
00:04:33,992 --> 00:04:35,190
Exam level now.

100
00:04:35,190 --> 00:04:38,622
Which of these four is a
disproportionation reaction?

101
00:04:38,622 --> 00:04:39,870
Pause and solve.

102
00:04:45,270 --> 00:04:46,727
The answer is B.

103
00:04:46,727 --> 00:04:49,030
Chlorine with sodium hydroxide.

104
00:04:49,530 --> 00:04:51,253
Chlorine starts at zero.

105
00:04:51,253 --> 00:04:56,156
It goes down to minus one in sodium
chloride, and up to plus one in sodium

106
00:04:56,156 --> 00:04:57,135
hypochlorite.

107
00:04:57,635 --> 00:04:59,000
A is decomposition.

108
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:00,315
C is displacement.

109
00:05:00,815 --> 00:05:02,895
D brings two forms of sulphur together.

110
00:05:02,895 --> 00:05:05,038
That is comproportionation, the opposite.

111
00:05:05,638 --> 00:05:07,178
Here is your recap.

112
00:05:07,178 --> 00:05:08,760
Screenshot this card.

113
00:05:11,960 --> 00:05:14,146
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

114
00:05:14,546 --> 00:05:15,446
One.

115
00:05:15,048 --> 00:05:18,485
Why is heating calcium
carbonate not a redox reaction?

116
00:05:19,285 --> 00:05:20,185
Two.

117
00:05:19,693 --> 00:05:21,956
Can copper push zinc out of zinc sulphate?

118
00:05:22,756 --> 00:05:23,656
Three.

119
00:05:23,299 --> 00:05:26,257
In disproportionation, what
happens to one element?

120
00:05:27,157 --> 00:05:31,256
Next time: how do you balance a
redox equation without guessing?

121
00:05:31,256 --> 00:05:35,646
We find out in balancing redox
reactions, the oxidation number method.
